.TH ENVSRV 4
.SH NAME
envsrv \- a sensor's readings of the room as files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount {envsrv
[
.B -d
.I dir
] [
.B -b
.I bus
] [
.B -a
.I addr
]
.B } /mnt/env
.br
.B mount {envsrv -t} /mnt/env
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Envsrv
serves the temperature, relative humidity and pressure a sensor
measures, one number to a file:
.TF humidity
.TP
.B temp
degrees Celsius, as
.BR 24.34 .
.TP
.B humidity
relative humidity in percent, as
.BR 57.4 ;
absent where the sensor measures none (a BMP280).
.TP
.B pressure
hectopascals, as
.BR 970.84 .
.TP
.B ctl
the sensor, as
.BR "chip=0x60 addr=0x76 bus=1 dir=#J" ,
.B chip=none
while none answers, or
.B sim .
.PD
.PP
A read of a value file at offset 0 is a measurement taken then, all
three values at once; reads within a second of it get its values, so
the three files read together are one measurement. A sensor that stops
answering is an error on the read, saying why, and the next read looks
for it again and reads its calibration again.
.PP
The sensor is a Bosch BME280, or a BMP280, on bus
.I bus
(1 by default) of the I2C files in
.I dir
.RB ( #J
by default), at
.I addr
or, with no
.BR -a ,
0x76 then 0x77. Another machine's
.BR #J ,
mounted, is read with
.BR -d .
.I Envsrv
looks for the sensor before it serves, and with none says so and
serves nothing.
.PP
With
.B -t
it simulates one, on any machine: the temperature swings six degrees
over the day (highest mid-afternoon, UTC), the humidity against it,
the pressure has its twice-daily tide and a slower drift, each with a
little noise. The values are a function of the time.
.PP
Another sensor is another server serving these names. Matrix's
.B history-svc
records them and
.B /lib/matrix/compositions/environment
draws a day of them.
.SH EXAMPLES
A board's sensor, read on another machine:
.IP
.EX
board; mount {envsrv} /mnt/env
board; listen -s tcp!*!17080 {export /mnt/env} &
viewer; mount tcp!board!17080 /mnt/env
viewer; cat /mnt/env/temp
24.34
viewer; wm/matrix /lib/matrix/compositions/environment
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/envsrv.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR camsrv (4),
.IR listen (1)
.SH BUGS
It takes the datasheet's floating-point compensation, one forced
measurement oversampled once, with no filter.
